"dolphin_browser.mst — the DD11 GOAL: a two-pane class browser over LIVE
 reflection data.

 `st_controls` proved a SysTreeView32 and a SysListView32 can be created and
 subclassed. That is the substrate. This is the thing the substrate was for,
 and it exercises what `st_controls` never touched: putting ITEMS in, letting
 a selection change drive the other pane, and reading back what Windows
 actually holds.

 THE DATA IS REAL. `ClassMirror allClasses` and `ClassMirror selectorsOf:` are
 the VM's own class API (st/world/76_reflection.mst) — the tree is a genuine
 slice of this image's class hierarchy and the list is a class's real
 selectors. A browser over a hard-coded array would pass every assertion here
 and prove nothing about reflection, which is half of what a class browser is.

 THE ASSERTIONS ASK WINDOWS, not the model, wherever Windows can be asked:
 TVM_GETCOUNT for the tree's item count and LVM_GETITEMCOUNT for the list's.
 A model correctly updated and a control that never received the items is
 exactly the failure this port keeps finding, and only the control can rule it
 out."
ShellView subclass: BrowserShell [
    | tree list text roots |

    tree [ ^tree ]
    list [ ^list ]
    text [ ^text ]

    BrowserShell class >> routeDolphinMessages [ ^UiSession routeMessagesFrom: View ]

    "The classes the tree shows. Deliberately a SMALL, NAMED slice rather than
     every class in the image: the assertions below count items, and a count
     that changes when someone adds a class to the world layer is a test that
     fails for the wrong reason.

     `Object` is the root and the rest are its real descendants here, so the
     hierarchy the tree draws is the hierarchy the image has — verified below
     by asking each class for its own `superclass` rather than assuming."
    browsedClasses [
        ^Array with: Magnitude with: Number with: Integer
    ]

    build [
        tree := self makeControl: TreeView.
        list := self makeControl: ListView.
        text := self makeControl: TextEdit.
        self layoutManager: (BorderLayout new
            west: tree;
            center: list;
            south: text;
            yourself).
        ^self
    ]

    makeControl: aViewClass [
        | v |
        v := aViewClass new.
        v parentView: self.
        v preferredExtent: 220 @ 240.
        v usePreferredExtent: true.
        self addSubView: v.
        ^v
    ]

    "── the tree pane ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Built through Dolphin's own `TreeModel>>add:asChildOf:`, with nil as the
     parent for a root. Each class is added under its own superclass when that
     superclass is in the slice, so the shape is derived, not stated."
    populateTree [
        | model classes |
        model := TreeModel new.
        classes := self browsedClasses.
        classes do: [ :each |
            model add: each asChildOf:
                ((classes includes: each superclass) ifTrue: [ each superclass ] ifFalse: [ nil ]) ].
        tree model: model.
        roots := model roots.
        ^model
    ]

    "── the list pane ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A class's own selectors, sorted so the order is an answer rather than an
     accident of the reflection primitive."
    selectorsFor: aClass [
        ^(ClassMirror selectorsOf: aClass) asSortedCollection asArray
    ]

    showClass: aClass [
        list list: (self selectorsFor: aClass).
        text text: aClass name , ' — ' , (self selectorsFor: aClass) size printString , ' selectors'.
        ^aClass
    ]

    "── what WINDOWS says is in each control ───────────────────────────────
     TVM_GETCOUNT = TV_FIRST(4352) + 5; LVM_GETITEMCOUNT = LV_FIRST(4096) + 4.
     Sent through the same `sendMessage:` the control views use, so a control
     whose handle is stale answers here rather than in a later assertion."
    treeItemCount [ ^User32 sendMessage: tree handle msg: 4357 wParam: 0 lParam: 0 ]
    listItemCount [ ^User32 sendMessage: list handle msg: 4100 wParam: 0 lParam: 0 ]

    "The tree's ROOT count, from the model — the shape assertion. With Object
     out of the slice, Magnitude is the only root and Number/Integer descend
     from it, so a flat tree and a correct one differ here."
    rootCount [ ^roots size ]
]
